          <p>
            Cordless battery models now exist, and they solve a different problem: washing things where there is no outlet, such as a boat at a slip or a car in a parking space. Most draw from a bucket or a hose and offer light pressure for short bursts. They are useful tools, but they do not replace either category above for house and driveway work.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are torn, list the three jobs you will do most often. If two of them are concrete or a whole-house wash, buy gas. If two of them are a car, a deck, siding or furniture, buy electric and add a surface cleaner for the occasional patio. Many households that bought gas for a once-a-year driveway would have been happier with electric and a pro visit every few years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="job-matching" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Matching the Machine to the Job
          </h2>

          <p>
            Different surfaces want very different amounts of force, and the right nozzle and distance matter as much as the machine. As a general guide, painted car finishes do best around 1,200 to 1,900 PSI with a 40-degree tip held a couple of feet away. Softwood decks such as pine and cedar want the low end, roughly 500 to 1,200 PSI, with a 25- or 40-degree tip. Vinyl and fiber cement siding sit around 1,300 to 1,600 PSI, sprayed downward, never up under the laps.
          </p>

          <p>
            Concrete is the surface that rewards power. Broom-finished driveways and sidewalks generally clean well at 2,500 to 3,500 PSI with good flow, ideally through a rotating surface cleaner that keeps the distance constant and stops striping. Pavers sit lower, roughly 1,500 to 2,500 PSI, so the jets do not blow out joint sand. Brick and stone vary by mortar condition, so start low and check the joints after the first pass.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Surface</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical PSI</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Tip</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
