                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flow matters most</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Stains are a separate problem from dirt. Oil, rust and tire marks do not respond much to more pressure; they respond to chemistry and dwell time. Treat oil with a degreaser or poultice first, then rinse, as covered in <a href="/how-to-remove-oil-stains-from-driveway/">removing oil stains from a driveway</a>. Mildew on siding and decks needs a cleaner that kills the growth, applied at low pressure through the soap nozzle and left to work before you rinse.
          </p>

          <p>
            If a single machine has to cover a car and a driveway, buy for the driveway and control the pressure for the car with a wide tip, distance and a foam cannon. The reverse does not work: no technique turns a 1.2 GPM electric into a fast concrete cleaner. The exception is a buyer whose concrete area is small, such as a front walk and a single-car pad, where an electric with a surface cleaner is entirely adequate.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pumps-engines" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Pumps and Engines: Where the Money Goes
          </h2>

          <p>
            The pump is the part that fails, and pump design largely decides how long a pressure washer lives. Most consumer machines use an axial cam pump, also called a wobble or swash plate pump, which is compact and inexpensive. It is designed for homeowner duty, commonly rated in the low hundreds of hours, and when it wears out it is usually replaced rather than repaired. That is a fair trade for someone who runs the machine a few weekends a year.
          </p>

          <p>
            Triplex plunger pumps use a crankshaft and three ceramic plungers, run at lower speeds, and can be rebuilt with seal kits and valves. Manufacturers commonly rate them for well over a thousand hours. They add several hundred dollars to the price, and they make sense for anyone who washes weekly, rents equipment, cleans for neighbors or runs a small side business. For the occasional user, the extra cost rarely pays back.
          </p>

          <p>
            Engines follow a similar split. Honda's GC series is a residential overhead-cam design used on many consumer pressure washers, while the GX series is the commercial line with a cast-iron cylinder sleeve built for long hours. Store-brand overhead-valve engines from Generac, Briggs and Stratton, Kohler and others are generally reliable on homeowner machines. What matters more than the badge is whether a local shop can get parts, so check before you buy an unfamiliar brand.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Rule</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Buy the pump for the hours you will actually run.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A few weekends a year: an axial cam pump is fine and the savings are real. Weekly use or paid work: a triplex pump pays for itself the first time it needs seals instead of replacement.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            On electrics, the motor is the comparable choice. Universal motors, common on budget units, are light and cheap but noisy and have carbon brushes that wear out. Induction and brushless motors run quieter and cooler and generally last longer. If you plan to keep a plug-in machine for many years, paying a little more for an induction or brushless design is worthwhile, as the higher-ranked electrics above reflect.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="nozzles-accessories" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Nozzles and Add-Ons Worth Buying
          </h2>

          <p>
            Quick-connect tips follow a color code. Red is a zero-degree pencil stream that concentrates all the force on a point the size of a coin. Yellow is 15 degrees, a narrow fan for stripping and tough spots. Green is 25 degrees, the general-purpose tip for most cleaning. White is 40 degrees, a wide, gentle fan for vehicles, windows and soft surfaces. Black is a low-pressure soap tip that opens the detergent injector. Most homeowners can leave red in the box permanently.
          </p>

          <p>
            A turbo or rotary nozzle spins a zero-degree stream in a fast circle, which gives you pencil-point cleaning power across a cone. It is excellent on bare concrete and stubborn stains and too aggressive for wood, siding and paint. Treat it as a concrete-only tool, keep it moving, and hold it farther back than you think you need to.
          </p>

          <p>
            The single best upgrade for anyone with concrete is a surface cleaner, a hooded spinning bar with two jets that rides on casters. It keeps distance constant, stops zebra stripes, contains spray, and often roughly halves the time a driveway takes. Match its rated PSI and GPM range to your machine; an oversized unit on a small electric just spins slowly. Our <a href="/best-surface-cleaner-for-pressure-washer/">surface cleaner guide</a> pairs models with machine classes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Other add-ons earn their keep in specific situations. A foam cannon is worth it for car washing because it clings and loosens dirt before you touch the paint. An extension wand lets you reach second-story siding from the ground, which is much safer than spraying from a ladder, where kickback can throw you off balance. A longer, non-kinking pressure hose saves constant repositioning on big jobs. Before buying any accessory, confirm the fitting size, usually 1/4-inch quick-connect for tips and either M22 or 3/8-inch for hoses.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="running-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Running It Safely and Getting Even Results
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gas engines produce carbon monoxide, an odorless gas that can kill in minutes in an enclosed space. Run a gas pressure washer outdoors only, never in a garage, shed or basement, even with the door open, and keep the exhaust pointed away from windows, doors and vents. This is the same guidance that applies to portable generators, and it is not optional. If anyone feels dizzy, nauseated or develops a headache, shut the engine off and get to fresh air.
          </p>

          <p>
            High-pressure water can cut skin and inject water and debris under it, an injury that looks minor and needs emergency care. Never point the wand at people or pets, never clear a clog with your hand, engage the trigger lock whenever you set the gun down, and wear closed shoes and eye protection. Plug electric units into a GFCI-protected outlet; most have an inline GFCI plug as well, and you should test it before each session.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before starting, check the water supply. A gas unit rated at 2.5 GPM needs a spigot that can deliver at least that. Time how long a 5-gallon bucket takes to fill from your hose: under two minutes means you have about 2.5 GPM or more. Starving the pump of water causes cavitation, pulsing pressure and fast wear. Run water through the pump before starting the engine or motor to purge air.
          </p>

          <p>
            For even results, start with the widest tip and step down only if needed. Hold the wand at a constant distance and move at a steady pace, overlapping each pass by about a third. Wash siding from the bottom up with detergent so it does not streak, then rinse from the top down. Do not leave the machine idling in bypass for more than a couple of minutes; recirculating water heats quickly and damages pump seals. Many machines have a thermal relief valve, but it is a backup, not a feature to rely on.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="storage-winter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Storage, Maintenance and Winter Prep
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pumps die from three things: running dry, freezing and mineral buildup. After each session, shut off the machine, squeeze the trigger to release trapped pressure, disconnect the hoses and let the pump drain. If you will not use it for a month or more, run a pump saver product through the inlet. It lubricates seals and leaves an antifreeze film inside the manifold, and it is the cheapest insurance you can buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Freezing is the most common way to destroy a pump. Water left inside expands as it freezes and cracks the manifold, and the damage often shows up only when you start the machine in spring. If the unit lives in an unheated garage or shed anywhere that sees freezing temperatures, winterize it in fall without exception. This applies equally to electric machines, which owners often assume are immune.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gas engines need fuel and oil care. Fill with fresh fuel and a stabilizer for the last run of the season, or run the carburetor dry, so ethanol blends do not varnish the jets over winter. Change the engine oil on the schedule in the engine manual, including the early break-in change many manufacturers specify. Check the spark plug and air filter yearly, and check the pump oil on models that have a separate pump crankcase.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hoses and fittings wear too. Inspect the high-pressure hose for bulges and abrasion before each season and replace any O-rings that weep at the quick connects. Store tips in a holder so the orifices stay clean; a partially blocked tip causes pulsing and uneven spray that owners often blame on the pump. A tip cleaning wire costs a few dollars and fixes most of those complaints.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
