        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the Red Tip on Anything You Care About</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The zero-degree tip concentrates the full force of the pump into a pinpoint. On wood it carves grooves, on concrete it can etch lines that show forever, and on skin it causes serious injection injuries. Start with the 40-degree tip, move closer before you move narrower, and keep the red tip for rare, isolated spots on bare metal or masonry.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spraying Up Under Siding Laps</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Siding is designed to shed water falling down, not water forced upward. Spraying from below drives water behind the panels into sheathing and insulation, where it feeds mold and rot you will not see for years. Hold the wand level or angled down, use detergent and a wide tip, and use an extension wand for upper stories.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on PSI Alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A big PSI figure looks impressive, but flow decides how fast you finish. A 3,000 PSI electric at 1.1 GPM delivers fewer cleaning units than a 2,700 PSI gas unit at 2.3 GPM by a wide margin. Multiply pressure by flow, check whether the figures are rated or peak, and buy for the job you do most.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the Engine in the Garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Starting a gas pressure washer just inside the garage door to stay out of the rain is a common way people are poisoned by carbon monoxide. The gas builds up quickly, has no smell, and drifts into the house. Run the engine outdoors only, well away from doors and windows, with the exhaust aimed away from the building.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing It Wet Over Winter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A single overnight freeze can crack a pump manifold, and the damage shows up only as a leak or no pressure next spring. Drain the pump, run pump saver antifreeze through it, and store it somewhere above freezing if possible. Electric units need exactly the same care; their pumps are just as vulnerable as gas pumps.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

