        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which pressure washer should most homeowners buy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a typical suburban home with a driveway, siding and a deck, the Simpson MegaShot MS60763 balances flow, engine reliability and price well. If your jobs are mostly cars, patios and siding, the Ryobi RY142300 brushless electric is easier to live with. Decide on your three most frequent jobs first, then choose the power source that suits them.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does flow or pressure matter more?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Flow, measured in GPM, is the one that predicts how fast you finish. PSI breaks dirt loose; flow carries it away. Multiply the two to get cleaning units, which is the most useful single figure for comparing machines. For delicate surfaces, moderate PSI with decent flow is the safest choice. For big concrete, prioritize GPM within your budget.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much PSI do I need for a concrete driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most broom-finished driveways clean well somewhere between 2,500 and 3,500 PSI, ideally with at least 2.3 GPM and a rotating surface cleaner. A smaller electric can clean concrete, but it takes much longer and is prone to striping. Pretreat oil and rust stains with the right cleaner, because extra pressure does little to lift them on its own.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a pressure washer damage my house or deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, easily. Too narrow a tip or holding the wand too close can splinter softwood, etch concrete, strip paint, force water behind siding and break window seals. Start with a wide tip at a distance, test in a hidden spot, and increase force only as needed. On older homes, painted surfaces or anything you are unsure about, low pressure and a cleaner are safer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are electric pressure washers good enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For cars, outdoor furniture, decks, siding and modest patios, a good electric is more than enough and much easier to own. Where they fall short is large concrete areas, because household circuits limit flow to under about 2 GPM. Pair an electric with a small surface cleaner and it will handle a walkway and a single-car pad comfortably.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a home pressure washer last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends mainly on the pump and how it is stored. Consumer axial cam pumps are typically rated in the low hundreds of hours, which can mean many years of occasional use if the machine is never frozen or run dry. Triplex pumps are commonly rated much higher and can be rebuilt. Winterizing and using pump saver extend life significantly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I rent or buy a pressure washer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you have one heavy job a year, renting a commercial gas unit for a day is usually cheaper and avoids storage and maintenance. Buying pays off once you have several substantial jobs each season or want to clean cars and furniture whenever you like. Many homeowners own an electric for routine work and rent gas for the yearly driveway.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
